WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:04.860
American Horror Story can be horrifying, and
it's made even more chilling when you consider

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:09.610
that many of the villains and victims across
its various stories are drawn from the real-life

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:13.440
crimes, both solved and unsolved, that populate
American history.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.180
The first season of American Horror Story
was retroactively dubbed Murder House.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:22.500
It's a fitting name for a season that saw
Connie Britton and Dylan McDermott star as

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:27.440
Vivien and Ben Harmon, a couple who move with
their teenage daughter Violet into a Los Angeles

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:27.940
mansion.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:32.540
As you can probably guess, the mansion is
haunted, and the Harmon's are constantly confronted

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:34.560
by the ghosts of those who have been murdered
there.

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:39.040
Among their many supernatural roommates is
Elizabeth Short, known to history as "The

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:39.760
Black Dahlia."

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:41.780
"I'm an actress.

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:43.520
I'm gonna make it big!

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:44.980
Everyone says so.

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:47.820
You can expect to see me on the silver screen
one day."

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:54.620
Mena Suvari played the AHS version of Short:
an aspiring actor, desperate to be famous,

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:58.440
who visits the house in the late '40s for
a dental appointment that turns lethal.

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:04.100
In reality, Short was indeed a Hollywood hopeful
who went missing in early January 1947.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:08.860
Her body was discovered six days later near
a park, horribly mutilated and posed in a

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:09.800
grotesque fashion.

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:14.320
The crime was never solved, though one police
detective has written a compelling book full

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:18.000
of evidence suggesting that his late father,
who was a doctor, was responsible.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:24.720
The second season of AHS was subtitled Asylum
and focused on the patients and staff of Briarcliff,

00:01:24.729 --> 00:01:27.810
a sanitarium in New England, during the 1960s.

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:30.050
One of the asylum's residents was Kit Walker.

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:35.420
He lived happily with his wife Alma until
the interracial couple was visited by extraterrestrials

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:36.260
who abducted her.

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:41.020
Kit's ensuing psychological breakdown leads
authorities to believe that he offed his missing

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:42.280
wife, and he's sent to Briarcliff.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:43.680
"Did you murder your wife?"

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:44.180
"No.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:46.120
And I'm not crazy."

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:47.120
"That's unfortunate.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.080
If they decide you're sane, you're going someplace
way worse than this."

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:56.200
Barney and Betty Hill were the model for Kit
and Alma's story, though they thankfully suffered

00:01:56.200 --> 00:02:00.800
no homicide allegations as a result of the
visitation they claim to have experienced

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:02.100
in 1961.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:06.530
While driving the backroads of New Hampshire,
they saw what Betty described as a shooting

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:09.410
star falling upwards that pursued their car.

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:14.090
Suddenly the pair found themselves 35 miles
from where they had been only moments earlier.

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:17.870
Their watches had stopped working, and they'd
lost two hours of time.

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:21.450
Under hypnosis, the couple claimed to recall
details of their visitation.

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:26.001
They were credible, as it seemed unlikely
that an interracial couple in the 1960s would

00:02:26.001 --> 00:02:28.260
want to draw further attention to themselves.

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:32.640
Betty claimed to continue to experience visitations
for years following her abduction.

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:38.200
New Orleans provides the magical setting for
Coven, the third installment of AHS, which

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:42.540
draws on the historical legacy of the Crescent
City to populate its cast of characters.

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:47.660
One of the show's most memorable monsters
is Madame Delphine LaLaurie, whose horrific

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:53.780
torture of her slaves reached levels of inhumanity
made even more ghastly by Kathy Bates' unsettling

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:56.460
performance and the real-life suffering that
inspired it.

00:02:56.680 --> 00:03:01.620
"It wasn't 150 slaves that died up here — I
can tell you that."

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:02.940
"Sorry?"

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:05.200
"It was 62."

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:11.160
Bates brings LaLaurie to life over 150 years
after the suspected death of the real Madame

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:14.380
Delphine, who was born in New Orleans in the
late 18th century.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:19.040
She lived a privileged life as a socialite
for a time, until reports began to emerge

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:21.440
that Delphine mistreated her slaves.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:25.600
The claims were not investigated, though the
city did have laws against such abuse.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:29.740
Following the suspected killing of a young
slave in her care, LaLaurie's house caught

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:35.120
fire in 1834 and revealed the family had indeed
brutalized those in her workforce — the

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:40.040
slaves had been starved, shackled at the neck
with spiked collars, and subjected to torture.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:45.720
Enraged by this cruelty, an angry mob ransacked
the house, and Delphine fled the city for

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:46.220
Paris.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:52.120
On AHS, Marie Laveau is a powerful, immortal
Voodoo Queen played by Angela Bassett, whose

00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:56.890
desire for revenge against Madame LaLaurie
leads her to form her own cult of followers.

00:03:56.890 --> 00:04:00.640
"Don't think that they didn't suffer, because
they did — greatly.

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:06.260
But the fate that I have planned for you will
make their suffering seem as a gentle sleep."

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:11.440
In the modern-day timeline of Coven, she alternately
makes and breaks alliances with the coven

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:15.980
of witches lead by Fiona Goode, descended
from the victims of the Salem Witch Trials.

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:20.140
Confirmed details of the life of the real
Marie Laveau, who was born to a creole mother

00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:23.370
and white father at the turn of the 19th century,
are scant.

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:27.830
The woman herself, a Catholic who wove threads
of African and Native-American traditions

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:32.499
into her religious practice, was reluctant
to tell her own story, according to her 1881

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:34.800
obituary in the New York Times.

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:40.540
What is known is that Laveau worked as a hairdresser,
a detail represented in her AHS counterpart,

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:44.939
and that this work informed her role as a
spiritual adviser to the wealthy and powerful

00:04:44.939 --> 00:04:49.110
people of New Orleans, who flocked to her
for guidance in matters both personal and

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:49.600
professional.

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:55.020
A traveling show of human curiosities star
in the fourth season of AHS.

00:04:55.020 --> 00:05:00.039
Led by scheming ringmaster Elsa Mars, the
carnival performers of AHS: Freak Show are

00:05:00.039 --> 00:05:03.969
misunderstood by the public, who perceive
them as monstrous because of their unique

00:05:03.969 --> 00:05:04.820
appearances.

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:09.839
Among them is Pepper, a microcephalic woman
recruited by Elsa after her family abandoned

00:05:09.839 --> 00:05:10.909
her to an orphanage.

00:05:10.909 --> 00:05:13.619
Her first appearance, however, wasn't in Freak
Show.

00:05:13.619 --> 00:05:18.259
She actually first popped up in Asylum, where
her storyline diverged from that of her historical

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:18.960
inspiration.

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:20.580
"Play with me!"

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:25.620
"Play with meee!"

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:32.260
Pepper, as played by Naomi Grossman in both
Asylum and Freak Show, was based on Schlitze

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:34.569
Surtees, a real carnival performer.

00:05:34.569 --> 00:05:40.029
Schlitze is thought to be a man named Simon
Metz, born in 1901 in Bronx, NY.

00:05:40.029 --> 00:05:44.360
His parentage is unknown, but his condition
relegated his brain development to that of

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:45.160
a young child.

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:49.990
He performed with the Ringling Brothers Circus
and at Coney Island in the late '20s before

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:54.289
being cast in Tod Browning's 1932 MGM cult
classic Freaks.

00:05:54.289 --> 00:05:59.200
He took the last name Surtees from his guardian
George, a former carnival barker and chimpanzee

00:05:59.200 --> 00:05:59.700
trainer.

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:03.980
Schlitze became one of the most well-known
carnival performers of his era, costumed in

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:05.460
a dress and billed as a woman.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:11.060
During the events of Freak Show, Elsa discovers
conjoined twins Dot and Bette Tattler and

00:06:11.069 --> 00:06:12.770
convinces them to join her troupe.

00:06:12.770 --> 00:06:17.360
Dot is reluctant, and relents only to please
her sister Bette, who dreams of becoming a

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:17.860
star.

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:21.560
Playing the role of two different sisters
on the same series would be a challenge for

00:06:21.569 --> 00:06:25.819
any actor, but Sarah Paulson's a pro — and
she successfully portrayed the twins with

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:26.980
ease and believability.

00:06:27.749 --> 00:06:32.860
"Elsa, you can be the fairy grandmother that
grants the wish of the plane Jane — that's

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:35.540
Dot — and transforms her into the epitome
of Glamour.

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:36.900
That's me."

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:42.099
The physiology of the Tattler sisters was
inspired by real-life conjoined sisters Abby

00:06:42.099 --> 00:06:47.369
and Brittany Hensel, whose 2003 documentary
Joined for Life was closely reviewed by Paulson

00:06:47.369 --> 00:06:48.870
in preparation for the part.

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:52.969
Yet the story of the Tattlers follows the
lives of a different set of twins: Violet

00:06:52.969 --> 00:06:54.040
and Daisy Hilton.

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:58.819
These conjoined sisters, like Bette and Dot,
suffered the abuse of their guardians during

00:06:58.819 --> 00:07:00.530
their early years in show business.

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:04.990
The pair toured the carnival circuit and even
starred with Schlitze Surtees in Freaks.

00:07:04.990 --> 00:07:08.929
The sisters saw great success for a time,
and eventually liberated themselves from their

00:07:08.929 --> 00:07:11.060
guardians on the advice of Harry Houdini.

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:15.180
Eventually, interest in their act dwindled
and they spent their last years working in

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:20.240
a grocery store before they passed away in
1969, dying a few days apart.

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:26.200
James March built the Hotel Cortez, where
the bloody events of AHS: Hotel take place.

00:07:26.210 --> 00:07:28.770
You see, March was no benevolent hotelier.

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:33.219
In fact, he specifically designed the place
around his violent tendencies, fashioning

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:36.840
rooms that enabled him to commit any number
of killings within its walls.

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:41.820
March is clearly modeled on real-life serial
killer H.H. Holmes, who did build a hotel

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:43.200
designed for homicide.

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:48.320
It featured gas jets, which turned guest rooms
into deadly chambers, and a wooden chute that

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:51.500
allowed for bodies to be swiftly disposed
of in an incinerator.

00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:55.849
His "murder hotel" claimed the lives of many
young women who visited there in connection

00:07:55.849 --> 00:07:58.340
with the 1893 Chicago World's Fair.

00:07:58.689 --> 00:08:02.860
When he was finally caught, Holmes confessed
to taking the lives of 30 people, though authorities

00:08:02.870 --> 00:08:06.209
believe he may have been responsible for nearly
200 deaths.

00:08:06.209 --> 00:08:09.100
He was hanged for his crimes in 1896.

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:13.750
His hotel was later intended to be remodeled
as a "Murder Castle" attraction but was destroyed

00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:15.040
by a fire before it's opening.

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:20.300
"Like the Iliad, your stories will live on…
forever."

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:25.620
The fourth episode of Hotel features a deadly
dinner party hosted by Mr. March.

00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:29.869
A who's who of famous criminals check in to
attend the festivities, which they've attended

00:08:29.869 --> 00:08:31.610
annually for many years.

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:35.820
Yup — the guests of March's party are some
of the most famous real serial killers in

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:36.760
American history.

00:08:36.930 --> 00:08:41.740
The first guest to check in is Richard Ramirez,
known as the Night Stalker, who was responsible

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:44.380
for at least 13 deaths in the 1980s.

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:48.800
The next killer to turn up at the hotel is
Aileen Wuornos , who confessed to the deaths

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:52.340
of six men she claimed assaulted her while
she worked as a prostitute.

00:08:52.649 --> 00:08:55.780
Jeffrey Dahmer, who killed at least 11 people,
also turns up.

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:59.820
Evidence indicates Dahmer may have consumed
some of the remains of his victims.

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:00.840
"Jeffrey?

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:04.060
You're even more quiet than usual tonight."

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:07.360
"I'm just hungry."

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:12.180
He's joined for dinner by none other than
John Wayne Gacy, who dressed as a clown in

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:15.220
his free time and also took the lives of 33
men.

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:21.280
In its sixth year, AHS decided to change things
up a bit and brought us Roanoke, which is

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:23.780
styled as a found-footage documentary.

00:09:24.029 --> 00:09:28.740
Roanoke — or, at least, its first half — follows
a young couple living in an old mansion known

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:32.740
as Roanoke House, near the site of the lost
Roanoke colony in Virginia.

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:37.860
Roanoke was a real colony from the 16th century
that represented the first attempt to establish

00:09:37.870 --> 00:09:39.790
an English settlement in North America.

00:09:39.790 --> 00:09:44.250
When the community's founder returned from
a two-year voyage, its inhabitants had all

00:09:44.250 --> 00:09:48.019
vanished, leaving the word "Croatoan" carved
into a tree nearby.

00:09:48.019 --> 00:09:53.149
Another nod to this history is series character
Tomasyn White, played by Kathy Bates's My

00:09:53.149 --> 00:09:56.400
Roanoke Nightmare character, Agnes Mary Winstead.

00:09:56.540 --> 00:10:01.320
White was a real person, and was indeed married
to John White, founder of the Roanoke colony.

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:07.860
"Were thou not birthed of mine own treacherous
flesh, I would cut thy throat and hurl thee

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:09.240
into bloody damnation."

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:14.540
While there's no evidence to suggest she accompanied
White to his colony in Virginia, their union

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:17.420
produced two children, including their daughter
Elizabeth.

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:22.180
Elizabeth bore a son at Roanoke, who vanished
along with his mother and father and the rest

00:10:22.180 --> 00:10:25.180
of the colony between 1588 and 1590.

00:10:25.940 --> 00:10:32.060
The 2016 U.S. Presidential election gave AHS
plenty of fuel for the story of Cult, their

00:10:32.069 --> 00:10:33.130
seventh season.

00:10:33.130 --> 00:10:37.200
In the turbulent post-election times, a cult
forms in a small town in Michigan.

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:41.820
One of its associates reveals she had a love
affair with none other than Valerie Solanas,

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:46.120
the woman who shot Andy Warhol, the famous
pop artist and leader of the studio known

00:10:46.120 --> 00:10:50.940
as the Factory, which drew artists and luminaries
of all kinds to congregate and experiment.

00:10:50.949 --> 00:10:55.380
In the AHS universe, Solanas was also responsible
for the Zodiac killings.

00:10:55.440 --> 00:11:00.280
In reality, that series of slayings was carried
out by an unknown person in the Bay area who

00:11:00.280 --> 00:11:05.300
taunted police with a series of cryptic messages
sent to local newspapers in the late '60s.

00:11:05.540 --> 00:11:09.960
Solanas only attempted to kill Warhol and
Mario Amaya, an art dealer.

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:14.329
Both men lived, but Warhol had to wear a surgical
corset for the rest of his life.

00:11:14.329 --> 00:11:17.310
The crime saw Solanas sentenced to three years
in prison.

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:21.940
She suffered from schizophrenic paranoia that
complicated her rehabilitation, and died in

00:11:21.940 --> 00:11:24.980
a welfare hotel in San Francisco in 1988.

